\n\n**Final Evaluation**\n\nIt is a basic strategic concept: I must prioritize defense in the face of imminent defeat, as a simple play at this stage is the only way to avoid immediate loss. It is my single and simple solution to this.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state presents a critical defensive situation for White. Black has established stones at G6, G8, and G9. There is a gap at G7. If Black plays at G7, they will form a continuous vertical line of four stones (G6, G7, G8, G9). Since the ends of this line at G5 and G10 are empty (indicated by dots), this formation would be an "Open 4" (or "Live 4"). An Open 4 is a guaranteed win because even if White blocks one end, Black can play the other to complete 5-in-a-row. White\'s own offensive prospects, such as the three stones in Column I (I7, I8, I9), are blocked at the top (I6 is Black) and do not allow for a winning sequence faster than Black\'s immediate threat.\n\nReason: White must prioritize survival by blocking Black\'s lethal threat in Column G. Playing at G7 prevents Black from forming an unstoppable Open 4. This move is forced; failing to play here will result in a loss on the next turn.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 7}'}
